You have no dagger, so you kneel and saw at the old rope with the edge of your longsword, careful of the man's wrists. The strands part. His skin is cold and clammy, but his pulse is steady under your fingers. You tilt his head, dribble water from your waterskin between his lips, and chafe his hands. He gasps and chokes and his eyes open wide.

He is a lean, sandy-haired man of perhaps thirty, caked in grey dust. He flinches from your light, then sees the dwarven holy symbol on your chest and sags with a sob. "Temple," he rasps. "Anvil's mercy. I'm Dorrin. Dorrin Pell." His eyes dart past you to the broken bones. "Did you get them both? There'll be more. The lights bring them up out of the floor."

You make him sip again. He tells you in broken gasps that he and two mates, Tamsin Vey and old Orrick, broke into a sealed hall and the lights came, small blue flames that walk on legs of bone, and herded them. He was left tied here as a spare. Tamsin and Orrick were taken down through a stair at the back of the chamber, to where the lights gather. That was a day and a night ago, and he has heard no cries since.

All this while you keep your shield up and your eyes on the far side of the chamber. A low stair, cut into the rock, leads down into the pale light at the back. Nothing has moved there yet. You have 2 hit points, and the sunrise bricking-up of the mine is your deadline.

What does Brakka do?


