You keep your voice low. Dorrin works his jaw, grips your forearm, and tries his legs. They hold, though he wobbles. "I can walk," he says. "Slowly. Won't run."

He answers what he can. The walking lights are small things, no higher than a dog, blue flames in cages of bone, perhaps six or seven of them. They gutter and bob about the walls and call the dead diggers up out of the floor. There is something taller, he says, in a foreman's coat gone green with rot, with a blue lamp where its head should be. It never speaks, but the lights go quiet when it turns. At the foot of the stair is a wide hall, which he calls the Lamp Hall. Tamsin and old Orrick were laid on stone slabs there, still breathing when he last saw them.

The chamber stays quiet. No tick of picks, no scrape of bone. You wedge a fallen prop across the gallery mouth, keep your shield on your arm and your eyes on the stair, and sit with Dorrin. You bind your side with strips of your own spare cloth, say the Anvil Father's short prayers, and eat a ration. An hour passes with only the drip of water. You rise rested, and the pain in your side is a dull ache. You have spent a hit die and regained 11 hit points, and your Second Wind has returned. You are at 13 of 14 hit points. It is a little after six in the evening.

Dorrin sits propped against the wall, grey-faced but alert. The stair waits, glowing faintly blue.

What does Brakka do?


